```csharp
public static bool AreAllCharactersUnique(string input)
{
    if (input.Length > 256) // If the string has more than 256 characters, it means there must be duplicates
        return false;

    int checker = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < input.Length; i++)
    {
        int value = input[i] - 'a'; // Assuming the input only contains lowercase alphabets
        int mask = 1 << value;

        if ((checker & mask) > 0) // If there is a bit set at the position, it means the character is repeated
            return false;

        checker |= mask; // Set the bit at the position to mark the character as seen
    }

    return true;
}
```

Explanation:
- We start by checking if the length of the input string is greater than 256. If it is, there must be duplicates as there are only 256 unique characters possible (ASCII range).
- We use an integer variable `checker` as a bit vector to keep track of the characters we have encountered so far.
- We iterate through each character of the string and calculate its value by subtracting 'a' from it. This assumes that the input only contains lowercase alphabets. If the input can contain uppercase or other characters, additional handling is required.
- We use the calculated value to create a bitmask by shifting 1 to the left by `value` positions.
- We check if the bit at the position of the bitmask in `checker` is already set. If it is, it means the character is repeated, and we return false.
- If the bit is not set, we set it by performing a bitwise OR operation between `checker` and the bitmask.
- Finally, if we have iterated through all the characters without finding any duplicates, we return true.

This approach has a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(1) as we are using a fixed-size integer variable `checker` to store the bitmask.

